2186124173 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124172 1075 17592186124174 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124172 1075 17592186124175 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124172 1075 17592186124176 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124172 1075 17592186124177 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124172 1075 17592186564982 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124172 1075 17592186564984 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124172 1075 17592186565066 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124172 1075 17592186565274 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124172 1075 17592186565325 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124172 1078 17592186565325 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124286 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124286 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\,$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124286 1074 "We can sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n1. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$.\n2. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124286 1074 "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124289 1071 "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124289 1071 "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124289 1074 "We can sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n1. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}$.\n2. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124289 1074 "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124386 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124386 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124386 1074 "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 4\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{4x^{4}} &= lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{x^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124386 1074 "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\dfrac 1 4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124387 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124387 1071 "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\,$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124387 1074 "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124387 1074 "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124452 1038 17592186565438 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124453 1038 17592186565477 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124457 1038 17592186565425 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124457 1038 17592186565432 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124475 1038 17592186565465 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124502 1038 17592186565460 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124506 1038 17592186565475 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124507 1038 17592186565414 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124507 1038 17592186565458 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124553 1038 17592186565416 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124553 1038 17592186565428 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124553 1038 17592186565429 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 17592186565424 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124558 1038 17592186565463 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124573 1038 17592186565411 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124573 1038 17592186565484 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124573 1038 17592186565485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124575 1038 17592186565412 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124575 1038 17592186565444 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124606 1038 17592186565434 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124611 1038 17592186565413 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124611 1038 17592186565461 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124626 1038 17592186565415 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124626 1038 17592186565476 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124627 1038 17592186565409 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124627 1038 17592186565422 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124627 1038 17592186565443 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124627 1038 17592186565447 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124705 1050 540.0 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124761 1070 17592186045486 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124761 1071 "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x, y, z) = x^{3} - yz$ on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a = ⟨0, 0, 0⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 1, 1⟩$, respectively." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124761 1071 "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively." 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124761 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2},-z,-y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{1} \\\\ x & =y=z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=t,\\,z=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-t-t & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(3t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(0,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}={c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124937 1070 17592186045485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124937 1071 "Find $lim_(x → (1, 1))\\frac{x^{2}y^{2}}{x^{3} + y^{3}}$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186124937 1071 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186124937 1074 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}+{1}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125015 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125015 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for\n$f(x, y) = y^{3} - x^{3}$ on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where the 