2)^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 4{c}_{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}-2=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125160 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125160 1071 "Compute $lim_(x → 1)(1)/((x - 1)^{2})$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125160 1071 "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125160 1074 "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125223 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125223 1071 "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125223 1071 "Find $lim_(x → (2, 2,-1))\\frac{xyz - zy^{2}}{y^{2} - x^{2}}$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125223 1074 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot {2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{xyz-z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz(x-y)}{-(x-y)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2,-1)}\\frac{yz}{-x-y} & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{-2-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125262 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125262 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x, y, z) = 4xz - y^{2} + z^{2}$ on the line segment $AB^(―)$, where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a = ⟨0, 1, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 3, 2⟩$, respectively." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125262 1071 "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩,$ respectively." 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125262 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3,2)-f(0,1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨0,1,1⟩) \\\\ 3-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4z,-2y,4x+2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{3}-4{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2t+1,\\,z=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=2t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3=4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-4(2t+1)+6(t+1) & =3 \\\\ 4t-8t-4+6t+6 & =3 \\\\ 2t & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{2})+1=2, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},2,\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125719 1064 "How do we calculate\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nTo find out, let's look at the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0\n$$\n\nThe same is true for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) = 0\\begin{vmatrix}andlim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{x^{2}}) = 0 \\\\ lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{x^{3}}) = 0\\end{vmatrix}andlim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{x^{3}}) = 0; ⋮\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\n$$\n\ndecreases." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125719 1064 "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases." 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125720 1064 "Let's again look at the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}\n$$\n\nAny function of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nand consequently the limit behavior is the same. For example,\n\n$$\nlim_(x → 0^{-})(\\frac{1}{x^{3}}) = - ∞,\\begin{vmatrix}lim_(x → 0^{+})(\\frac{1}{x^{3}}) = ∞, \\\\ lim_(x → 0^{-})(\\frac{1}{x^{5}}) = - ∞,\\end{vmatrix}lim_(x → 0^{+})(\\frac{1}{x^{5}}) = ∞,; ⋮\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125720 1064 "Let's again look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n},$ where $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ and consequently the limit behavior is the same.  For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=\\infty , \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same." 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125721 1064 "Now let's look at the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty\n$$\n\nAny function of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}\n$$\n\n(where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125721 1064 "Now let's look at the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ (where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125756 1064 "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nThis is because as $x$ increases, any polynomial $P(x)$ will grow without bound, and therefore its reciprocal shrinks to zero.\n\nFor example, the expression $x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^3 - x^2 + 1}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nLikewise, the expression $(2x-5)^4$ can be multiplied out to become a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{(2x-5)^4}\\right) = 0\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186125756 1064 "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is because as $x$ increases, any polynomial $P(x)$ will grow without bound, and therefore its reciprocal shrinks to zero.\n\nFor example, the expression $x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^3 - x^2 + 1}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the expression $(2x-5)^4$ can be multiplied out to become a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{ (2x-5)^4 }\\right)  = 0.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1075 17592186126615 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186126614 1075 17592186126616 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186126614 1075 17592186126617 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186126614 1075 17592186126618 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186126614 1075 17592186126619 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186126614 1075 17592186564976 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1075 17592186565000 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1075 17592186565010 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1075 17592186565131 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1075 17592186565211 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186126614 1078 17592186565010 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129153 1038 17592186565436 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129153 1038 17592186565468 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129153 1038 17592186565473 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129267 1038 17592186565440 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129268 1038 17592186565439 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129519 1070 17592186045486 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129519 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): (x - 1)^{2} + y^{2} \\le 1, y \\ge 0, 1 \\le z \\le 2}$,\nthen $∭_(R)x^{2} + y^{2}dV =$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186129519 1071 "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V=$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186129519 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/60/603dc3142d8329fd9ff9fe8d09b131fe09497405f761918d59261fde314772c6.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $1$ whose axis passes through the point $(1,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186134101 1068 17592186130762 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186134101 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186134101 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186147445 1038 17592186565427 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186147445 1038 17592186565433 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186147446 1038 17592186565431 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186147462 1038 17592186565469 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186147462 1038 17592186565479 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186147462 1038 17592186565483 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186148913 1078 17592186148918 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152148 1038 17592186565430 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152148 1038 17592186565455 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152149 1038 17592186565423 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152231 1038 17592186565419 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152231 1038 17592186565462 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152397 1070 17592186045486 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152397 1071 "Let $R = [0, 4] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 2x + 3y + 1$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 2, 4}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186152397 1071 "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186152397 1074 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff5612495e4f64e710963ca4d4fc6b7b259164c7ef1943aa37467a71c1d48353.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{21}+2{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=2x+3y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ff/ffa7288d56b6604cf0ad013941003642209cf8ae75eca22d791cc5b65df4de35.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](images/be/be946433e503c6d7125fd53de795b5606666f20f3aa6164dd7e7b947b08af6d6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,0)=5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(0,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186159710 1075 17592186159712 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186159710 1075 17592186159715 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186159710 1075 17592186565028 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186159710 1075 17592186565117 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186159710 1078 17592186159711 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 17592186565451 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186162418 1070 17592186045485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186162418 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y)$, where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$S_{X} = {1, 2, 3, 4}, S_{Y} = {0, 1, 2, 3}$.\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 4, y > 3$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186162418 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>4,y>3.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186162418 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4\\}\\times \\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>4$ and $y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point. \n\n![](images/57/575107f600fc73bfa39978f161d9ba9da2ec9315d7be4487e7069ceb4ac590c6.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $4$ and $x>4,$ every value in the support of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $3$ and $y>3,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186165189 1078 17592186165194 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 17592186565421 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186166752 1038 17592186565478 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186166753 1038 17592186565420 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186166753 1038 17592186565445 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186167330 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186167330 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186167330 1071 "Consider the following matrices:\n$B_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, B_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf $B = {B_{1}, B_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{B_{1}, B_{2}}$, and $[A]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$, then $x_{1} + x_{2} =$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186167330 1074 "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 7 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{7}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=-2 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186175993 1038 17592186565486 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186177537 1078 17592186177541 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186177549 1075 17592186177553 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186177549 1075 17592186565267 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186177549 1078 17592186177550 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179720 1038 17592186565441 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179720 1038 17592186565464 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179720 1038 17592186565470 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179721 1038 17592186565410 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179721 1038 17592186565453 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179721 1038 17592186565472 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179723 1038 17592186565442 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179723 1038 17592186565480 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179978 1070 17592186045486 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179978 1071 "Let $P = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}$ be a partition of the interval $[0, 4]$. If $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, calculate $∑_(j = 1)^(n)8(y_{j} + y_{j - 1})Δy_{j}$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186179978 1071 "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[0,4].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}8({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179978 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}8({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}=8{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})Δ{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})Δ{y}_{2}+⋯+({y}_{n}+{y}_{n-1})Δ{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})({y}_{1}-{y}_{0})+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})+⋯+({y}_{n}+{y}_{n-1})({y}_{n}-{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${y}_{0}=0$ and ${y}_{n}=4$ are the endpoints of our partition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n8{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}=8\\cdot 16=128.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179980 1070 17592186045485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179980 1071 "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[- 2, 6]$?\n\n1. ${-2,-1, 0, 6}$\n2. ${0, 2, 4, 6}$\n3. $-2 < 2 < 4 < 5$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186179980 1071 "Which of the following are partitions of the real interval $[-2,6]?$\n\nI. $\\{-2,-1,0,6\\}$\n\nII. $\\{0,2,4,6\\}$\n\nIII. $-2<2<4<5$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186179980 1074 "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na={x}_{0}<{x}_{1}<{x}_{2}<…<{x}_{n}=b\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can express $P$ using set notation as\n\n$$\nP=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{n}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement:\n\n- Statement I is a partition of $[-2,6].$\n\n- Statement II is not a partition of $[-2,6]$ since it does not include the left endpoint $a=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is not a partition of $[-2,6]$ since it does not include the right endpoint $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1075 17592186189533 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186189532 1075 17592186189534 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186189532 1075 17592186189535 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186189532 1075 17592186189536 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186189532 1075 17592186189537 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186189532 1075 17592186564945 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1075 17592186565042 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1075 17592186565185 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1075 17592186565214 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1075 17592186565297 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186189532 1078 17592186564945 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1038 17592186565426 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192823 1038 17592186565450 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1038 17592186565435 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192824 1038 17592186565471 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192941 1070 17592186045485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192941 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{7 - 3x}{2} > 2$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186192941 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{7-3x}{2}>2.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186192941 1074 "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7-3x}{2} & >2 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{2}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7-3x}{\\cancel{2}}) & >2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 7-3x & >4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-3x & >4 \\\\ 7-3x-7 & >4-7 \\\\ -3x & >-3 \\\\ \\frac{-3x}{-3} & \\,\\,<\\,\\,\\frac{-3}{-3} \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<1.$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186203497 1078 17592186203502 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186204598 1070 17592186045485 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186204598 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px = 3x + 5$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?" 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186204598 1071 "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2px=3x+5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186204598 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $3x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2px & =3x+5 \\\\ 2px-3x & =3x+5-3x \\\\ 2px-3x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(2p-3)}_{⏟}}_{a}x={{5}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2p-3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p-3 & =0 \\\\ 2p-3+3 & =0+3 \\\\ 2p & =3 \\\\ \\frac{2p}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1075 17592186228661 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186228660 1075 17592186228662 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186228660 1075 17592186228663 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186228660 1075 17592186228664 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186228660 1075 17592186228665 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186228660 1075 17592186564962 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1075 17592186565123 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1075 17592186565195 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1075 17592186565204 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1075 17592186565288 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186228660 1078 17592186565195 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229171 1038 17592186565481 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229172 1038 17592186565448 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229172 1038 17592186565466 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229740 1070 17592186045487 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229740 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y={u}_{1}(t)\\cdot {e}^{-2(t-1)}\\,,\\,y(0)=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}(t)=u(t-1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{{e}^{-s}}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186229740 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$\\frac{dy}{dt} + y = u_{1}(t) \\cdot e^{-2(t - 1)}, y(0) = 1$,\nwhere $u_{1}(t) = u(t - 1)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = L{y(t)}$ is\n$Y(s) = \\frac{1}{s + 1} + (e^{-s})/((s + 1)(s + 2))$\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as\n$y(t) = e^{-t} + u(t - 1) \\cdot g(t)$.\nFind the function $g(t)$." 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186229740 1074 "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains distinct linear factors, the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+1}+\\frac{B}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=A(s+2)+B(s+1)\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Setting $s=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(-1+2)+B(0)\\,⟹\\,A=1.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(-2+1)\\,⟹\\,B=-1.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+1}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-1)f(t-1) & =u(t-1)[{e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{-t}+u(t-1)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)={e}^{-(t-1)}-{e}^{-2(t-1)}.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1075 17592186281915 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1075 17592186282181 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1075 17592186282645 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1075 17592186283308 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1075 17592186283588 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1075 17592186564991 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1075 17592186565044 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1075 17592186565095 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1075 17592186565145 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1075 17592186565188 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284357 1078 17592186283588 13194139536086 17592186045417 false] [17592186284357 1078 17592186565095 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284472 1074 "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}=1$ and its boundary. \n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.\n$$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186284472 1074 "Fro